// A local initialised FROM a borrowed parameter keeps borrowing. Without that,
// the read-only default was decorative: `m := a` shares `a`'s reference, so
// `m[0] = 99` reached the caller's array through a one-line detour — it compiled,
// and the mutation took effect (verified: `xs[0]=99 len=4`).
//
// A local initialised from an OWNED value stays mutable, which is the whole point
// of "a local is owned" — see snippets/const_binding_no_mutate.
//
// Requalified: the rejection now comes from the TYPE, not from a flow predicate.
// `a` desugars to `const i32[]`, `m := a` inherits that type, and the ordinary
// array-mutation check fires — T3042 instead of T3070, same position, same
// rejection, and no dedicated "did the borrow leak into a local" analysis. This
// is exactly what "the bit travels on its own" buys.
module "snippet"

sneak :: fn(a: i32[]) -> void {
    m := a
    m[0] = 99
}

main :: fn() -> i32 {
    xs :: [1, 2, 3]
    sneak(xs)
    return xs[0]
}
